.TH END 3
.SH NAME
end, etext, edata \- last locations in program
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B extern char end[];
.br
.B extern char etext[];
.br
.B extern char edata[];
.SH DESCRIPTION
These names refer neither to routines
nor to locations with interesting contents.
The address of
.I etext
is the first address above the program text,
.I edata
above the initialized data region, and
.I end
above the uninitialized data region.
.PP
When execution begins, the program break
coincides with
.I end,
but many functions reset the program break, among them
the routines of
.IR brk (2),
.IR malloc (3)
and
standard input/output
.RI ( stdio (3)).
The current value of the program break
is reliably returned by `sbrk(0)',
see
.IR brk (2).
.B Note:
v7 also listed the profiling option of
.IR cc (1);
there is no profiling runtime here.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
brk(2), malloc(3)
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS The segment order is const, text, data, bss
Four segments, not the PDP-11's three: a
.I const
segment holds string literals and initialized file-scope arrays, ahead of the
text.  That is why the size check on a program sums
.B const+text+data+bss
and why a
.B .dis
listing begins below the entry point.
.SS Declare it as an array
.B "extern char end[];"
and not
.BR "extern char end;" .
The decay of an array name produces a genuine fat
.B char *
at byte offset 0, where taking the address of a
.B char
object would produce one describing that one byte.
.IR Sbrk (3)
declares it exactly that way for exactly that reason.
.SS bss is the segment that can outrun a short address
An address field in an instruction reaches [0..07777] and [070000..077777]; the
segment order above puts bss last, so it is the one segment a large program can
push past the 12-bit field.  Assembly that names a bss symbol uses the
.B "< >"
address escape;
.I crt0
and
.I cerror
both say so at the point where they do it.
.PP
A separate ceiling applies to the whole program: text plus data plus bss must fit
below the stack at 070000 \- 28,672 words \- and no relocatable symbol may sit
above word 32,767, the reach of a 15-bit pointer.  Both failures are silent, so
the build checks them with
.BR "b6size \-w" .
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/crt0.o, lib/libc/csu/crt0.s
